<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henri Christophe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Henri I of Haiti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henri Christophe was a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a>. After Dessalines was killed in 1806 he rejected the weak presidency offered by the southern elite and set up the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in the north, proclaiming himself King Henri I in 1811. He built the palace of Sans-Souci and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere">Citadelle Laferrière</a>, and shot himself in 1820 after a stroke and an army mutiny.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-christophe.jpg" alt="Henri Christophe" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Evans · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henri_Christophe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dessalines, Emperor Jacques I, had 3,000 to 5,000 remaining whites killed and was assassinated in 1806; <b>Henri Christophe</b>'s northern kingdom and Alexandre Pétion's southern republic were reunited by Jean-Pierre Boyer in 1820, who conquered Santo Domingo in 1822.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henri Christophe</b>, the senior general and commander of the north, was elected president under this charter but rejected its limits and marched on Port-au-Prince, where he was repulsed in January 1807.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Haiti then split between <b>Henri Christophe's</b> State of Haiti in the north and Alexandre Pétion's Republic of Haiti in the south.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-empire-of-haiti">First Empire of Haiti</a></li><li><blockquote>The State of Haiti in the north became a kingdom in March 1811, when a council of state proclaimed <b>Henri Christophe</b> king.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-haiti">Kingdom of Haiti</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henri Christophe</b>, the senior general in the north, refused to accept it, marched on the capital and was repulsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-haiti">State of Haiti</a></li><li><blockquote>Facing pressure from <b>Henri Christophe</b>'s Kingdom of Haiti, Borgella reconciled with Pétion, and the south rejoined the Republic of Haiti in March 1812.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-south-haiti">State of South Haiti</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Haïtien</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.32,-62.75&amp;year=1801" aria-label="Saint Kitts on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2845 1521 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henri Christophe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3295 1791h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3295 1791h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.32,-62.75&amp;year=1801">What was here in 1801? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Go and tell your General that the French shall march here only over ashes, and that the ground shall burn beneath their feet.&#8221;</p><footer>Reply to General Leclerc's envoy at Cap-Français, 1802 &#183; tr. John Relly Beard, 1863 &#183; as reported in J. R. Beard's biography of Toussaint Louverture &#183; <a href="https://docsouth.unc.edu/neh/beard63/beard63.html" rel="noopener">Documenting the American South, UNC</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Since you have no letters for the General-in-chief nor for me, you may return and tell your general that he does not know his duty; that it is not thus that people present themselves in a country belonging to France.&#8221;</p><footer>Reply to General Leclerc's officer at Cap-Français, 1802 &#183; tr. John Relly Beard, 1863 &#183; as reported in J. R. Beard's biography of Toussaint Louverture &#183; <a href="https://docsouth.unc.edu/neh/beard63/beard63.html" rel="noopener">Documenting the American South, UNC</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
